B=[]; I=[]
def course(target,slug,title,summary,project,projectcriteria,lessons,optional=False,dep=None,hours=18):
 target.append(dict(slug=slug,title=title,summary=summary,project=project,projectcriteria=projectcriteria,lessons=lessons,optional=optional,dep=dep,hours=hours))
def L(title,concept,r,focus,lab,evidence,challenge,q,answer):return (title,concept,r,focus,lab,evidence,challenge,q,answer)
course(B,'python','Python execution and concurrency','Reason about interpreter execution, task lifetimes and the difference between concurrency and parallelism.',
'Concurrency laboratory: build a CLI that runs the same CPU-heavy checksum and delayed HTTP workload sequentially, in threads, in processes and with asyncio. Add cancellation and bounded admission; publish measured throughput, memory and tail latency.',
['For 1, 10 and 100 in-flight jobs, retain raw timings and explain the saturation point.','Cancel a run halfway through: no child process, socket or task remains after shutdown.','A CPU-bound task cannot silently block the asynchronous scheduler; demonstrate detection and isolation.'],[
L('Execution, ownership and the interpreter','Python names reference objects; assignment does not copy mutable state. A closure can retain an object beyond its apparent scope, and reference counting is not a substitute for explicit resource cleanup. CPython execution constraints also depend on interpreter build: a conventional GIL-enabled build serializes much Python bytecode, while free-threaded builds have different tradeoffs. Measure your actual runtime instead of treating threads as universally parallel or universally useless. Context managers make file and lock lifetimes visible even when an exception interrupts control flow.','python','Read binding, resolution and runtime interaction. Then inspect the runtime documentation for the GIL of your installed interpreter.','Trace a shared mutable default, a late-bound closure and a file handle escaping a context manager; repair all three.','Tests show independent per-call state, the intended closure values and a closed handle after an exception.','Compare a CPU loop with a native hashing operation in a thread pool; record Python build and CPU utilization.','Why can two threads race even when a GIL exists?','The GIL does not make a multi-step read/modify/write invariant atomic, nor protect external side effects. Synchronize the invariant or transfer ownership.'),
L('Structured concurrency and backpressure','An asynchronous function runs until it suspends; adding async to a CPU loop does not create cooperation. A TaskGroup gives related tasks a shared lifetime, but cancellation still requires cleanup in finally blocks. A semaphore bounds active work, while an unbounded queue can still accumulate unlimited waiting work. Bound both admission and execution, propagate an absolute deadline, and distinguish a timed-out caller from an operation that may already have committed.','async','Read TaskGroup, cancellation, timeout and shield. Sketch which exception escapes when a sibling task fails.','Implement a bounded producer/consumer pipeline with a TaskGroup, queue capacity 20 and five workers.','Queue depth never exceeds 20; a worker failure cancels siblings and closes all resources.','Add a 200 ms end-to-end deadline and a deliberately blocking function; detect scheduler delay with a heartbeat.','Why is a semaphore alone insufficient during overload?','It limits running operations, not the number or memory footprint of waiting callers. Admission must be bounded and rejection explicit.')])
course(B,'linux','Operating systems and Linux diagnosis','Connect process, memory, file descriptor and scheduling behavior to symptoms seen in Python services.',
'Build a Linux incident laboratory with reproducible modes for CPU saturation, leaked descriptors, growing resident memory and blocked I/O. Supply a diagnostic runbook using proc, ps, ss and strace.',
['Identify the correct failure using evidence from two independent OS signals.','Each failure has a bounded trigger and a cleanup command.','Distinguish virtual memory, resident memory and container memory limits in the report.'],[
L('Processes, virtual memory and resource limits','A process owns an address space and resources; threads share much of that space while retaining execution state. Virtual allocation is not equal to resident RAM, and high RSS is not automatically a leak. Page faults, allocator retention and copy-on-write change what a memory chart means. File descriptors are finite handles shared through inheritance and duplication. Diagnose the resource that is exhausted before changing worker counts: more workers can multiply connection pools and memory pressure.','os','Read Processes, Address Spaces and Paging; derive what fork shares before either process writes.','Compare RSS and descriptor counts while repeatedly allocating buffers and opening files, then closing and releasing them.','Record PID, RSS, virtual size and descriptor count before/after; explain which changes persist.','Run the workload under a low descriptor limit and a memory limit, then make failure recoverable.','Why does adding four workers increase database connections?','Each process normally creates its own pool. Aggregate capacity is workers times per-process pool size plus administrative clients.'),
L('Diagnosing blocked work on Linux','A slow endpoint can be computing, waiting for a lock, waiting for a socket or waiting to be scheduled. CPU utilization alone cannot distinguish these paths. Inspect process state, open sockets, syscall durations and per-thread stacks while preserving a timestamped hypothesis. Linux signals coordinate shutdown but cannot guarantee cleanup after SIGKILL. The service must stop admission, drain bounded work and close resources within the orchestrator grace period.','linux','Read status, fd, limits and task entries; map each to a production question.','Use strace with elapsed syscall timing and ss to distinguish a sleeping worker from a connection stall.','A timestamped trace identifies the blocking syscall or wait; SIGTERM drains a running request within five seconds.','Introduce a lock convoy and diagnose it without inserting print statements into every function.','What can a high load average with low CPU mean?','Runnable work and uninterruptible waits can contribute; inspect process states and I/O rather than assuming CPU saturation.')])
course(B,'network','Networking and HTTP under failure','Understand byte streams, connection setup, proxy behavior and API semantics under retries.',
'Build an instrumented reverse-proxy experiment around an existing Flask service. Simulate slow reads, dropped connections and duplicate POSTs; implement explicit connection/read budgets and conditional updates.',
['A fragmented request body is decoded without assuming one recv returns one message.','A retried operation uses an idempotency key with a persisted result.','A stale If-Match update is rejected and proxy timeout behavior is documented.'],[
L('TCP, DNS, TLS and connection budgets','TCP is an ordered byte stream rather than a message queue: a read can return part of a message or several messages. DNS, TCP establishment, TLS negotiation, pool waiting and response reads consume different parts of a request budget. Reusing connections avoids setup work but can preserve stale destinations. A timeout is an observation by one participant, not proof that the peer did no work. Instrument phases and propagate remaining time instead of assigning every hop an independent full timeout.','net','Read send, recv, partial I/O and closing connections; draw DNS/TCP/TLS/request timing.','Write a length-prefixed echo client/server that fragments each write and enforces maximum frame length.','Tests split the header and body at every byte boundary and reject oversized frames before allocation.','Add DNS delay and a slow peer; report which phase consumes the deadline.','Why is a successful send not evidence the request committed?','It may only mean bytes entered a local buffer. Application acknowledgement and durable server state establish the result.'),
L('HTTP semantics, proxies and safe retries','HTTP methods communicate semantics to clients and intermediaries. Safe, idempotent and cacheable are separate properties. An idempotent method can return different responses while preserving the same intended server effect. Conditional requests prevent lost updates, while idempotency keys handle repeated commands when implemented with durable deduplication. Proxy buffering and timeout policies can hide how much work the origin completed. Always reason about an ambiguous response after a connection loss.','http','Read sections 9, 13 and 15 on methods, preconditions and status codes; derive PUT versus POST retry behavior.','Implement ETag/If-Match updates and an idempotency-key POST over a small inventory resource.','Two clients using one ETag cannot both overwrite the resource; a duplicate key returns the original result.','Drop the response after commit and retry; reject the same key with a different payload.','Does idempotent mean identical response bytes?','No. It constrains the intended effect of repetition, not every observable response field or access log entry.')])
course(B,'security','API security and trust boundaries','Protect identities, resources and downstream systems with explicit authorization and bounded inputs.',
'Threat-model a multi-tenant document API. Implement object-level authorization, resource budgets and token validation; test cross-tenant access, SSRF-like inputs and leaked-secret handling.',
['Every read/write checks tenant membership using server-derived identity.','Invalid issuer, audience and expired credentials are rejected.','Tests prove URL validation blocks internal destinations after resolution and redirect handling.'],[
L('Authentication is not object authorization','Authentication establishes a principal; authorization answers whether that principal may perform this action on this object now. Looking up an object by an unpredictable ID does not authorize access. Apply tenant scoping before returning data and before mutating it, including background jobs and exports. Limits on payload size, pagination and expensive operations defend availability as well as cost. Audit the decision without recording raw bearer tokens or private document contents.','owasp','Read API1, API4 and API7; list an attack path for each in a tenant-aware API.','Create a two-tenant fixture and test every object endpoint using the other tenant’s IDs.','The authorization matrix covers owner, collaborator, outsider and revoked membership for reads and writes.','Add an export job and prove its worker rechecks access rather than trusting queued IDs.','Why is filtering only the list endpoint insufficient?','Attackers can call item endpoints or indirect operations directly. Every access path must enforce the same object policy.'),
L('Tokens, sessions and downstream trust','A signed token is useful only if the receiver validates issuer, audience, expiry and the intended algorithm using trusted configuration. Access tokens are not arbitrary credentials to forward to another resource. OAuth delegates authority; it does not replace application authorization. PKCE protects authorization-code interception, while secure session handling must also consider CSRF, replay and credential rotation. A trusted backend can still become an SSRF proxy if users control downstream addresses.','oauth','Read redirect validation, PKCE, audience restrictions and token replay mitigations; diagram trust at each hop.','Build a token-validation test matrix and a URL-fetch policy with scheme, destination and redirect restrictions.','Wrong audience, expired tokens and redirects to private IP space all fail closed.','Rotate signing keys while concurrent requests continue; document cache refresh and failure policy.','Why should an API not accept a token issued for a different service?','Its audience binds authority to the intended resource. Accepting it creates a confused-deputy path across trust boundaries.')])
course(B,'transactions','Relational design and transactional APIs','Model invariants and enforce them under concurrent updates rather than relying on happy-path application checks.',
'Build a reservation API in PostgreSQL with stock limits, cancellation and idempotent commands. Include a concurrent test driver and schema migration; no overselling is permitted.',
['100 concurrent requests for ten units yield exactly ten reservations.','A retried command cannot create a second reservation or charge record.','Document isolation, lock ordering and the transaction boundary with rollback tests.'],[
L('Constraints, isolation and write anomalies','A preflight SELECT followed by an UPDATE is not an invariant when other transactions can run between them. Place constraints in the database where possible and choose a transaction strategy for the remaining rules. Read committed gives each statement a fresh snapshot; stronger isolation changes which anomalies are possible and may require retries. Locks coordinate contenders but can deadlock when acquired inconsistently. Treat a serialization failure as a request to retry the entire transaction, including its reads.','pg','Read transaction isolation, row-level locks and deadlocks; reproduce the documented serialization example.','Run two sessions that try to reserve the final stock unit using check-then-write, then repair with a conditional UPDATE.','A barrier-controlled test reproduces overselling before the fix and prevents it afterward.','Implement an alternative serializable transaction with bounded full-transaction retries.','Why is retrying only the failed SQL statement wrong after serialization failure?','Earlier reads and decisions may no longer be valid. The transaction must restart from a new snapshot.'),
L('Schema evolution and transaction boundaries','Normalization separates facts so updates do not leave contradictory copies; denormalization trades maintenance work for read efficiency. Constraints encode local truths, while business workflows span multiple statements and sometimes multiple systems. Evolve a live schema using expand, backfill and contract steps so old and new application versions can overlap. Never hold a database transaction open while waiting indefinitely for an external payment service; commit an intent and coordinate the remote effect explicitly.','pg','Read consistency checks and application-level consistency; contrast constraint enforcement with transaction isolation.','Migrate a populated orders table to a normalized currency/amount representation without a flag-day deployment.','Old and new writers coexist during backfill; invalid amounts fail at the database boundary.','Add payment intent and reconciliation states without holding a transaction during a remote call.','Where should an external side effect occur relative to commit?','It cannot be atomically included in a local transaction by timing alone. Persist intent, use an outbox or reconciliation, and deduplicate the remote action.')])
course(B,'postgres','PostgreSQL internals and query performance','Use plans, indexes, visibility and WAL evidence to explain database behavior.',
'Create a million-row event workload with skewed tenants. Diagnose one slow query, one update-heavy table and one pool-saturation incident; publish before/after plans and maintenance decisions.',
['Capture EXPLAIN ANALYZE with buffers for cold and warm runs and acknowledge measurement effects.','Explain estimated versus actual row-count mismatch and choose an index with write-cost evidence.','Demonstrate dead-tuple growth and recovery after a long transaction is released.'],[
L('Plans, indexes and cardinality','A query planner chooses a plan from cost estimates based on statistics, selectivity and available access paths. An index is not automatically faster: fetching many scattered heap pages can cost more than scanning. Composite B-tree order affects which predicates efficiently narrow the scan, while covering indexes still interact with visibility. EXPLAIN ANALYZE executes the query, so mutating statements need deliberate rollback and operational care. Compare row estimates to actual rows before treating an expensive node as the root cause.','plan','Read plan trees, join examples, buffers and caveats; annotate one real plan from leaf to root.','Load skewed events and compare tenant/time queries before and after composite indexes and ANALYZE.','Retain plans, row estimates, buffer counts, data size and insert throughput for each index variant.','Introduce a correlated predicate that misleads estimates and evaluate extended statistics.','Why can the planner reject an available index?','For a large fraction of rows, sequential access may be cheaper. Statistics, correlation, cache and random access costs affect the choice.'),
L('MVCC, vacuum, WAL and connection pools','MVCC keeps row versions so readers can observe snapshots while writers proceed. Updates create maintenance work: dead versions cannot be reclaimed while old snapshots still need them. Vacuum and analyze solve different problems, and long transactions can undermine both operational expectations and storage reuse. WAL records changes before data pages are safely persisted; checkpoints and replication introduce separate I/O and lag concerns. A pool is an admission boundary, not a way to make the database handle unlimited concurrent work.','vacuum','Read vacuum purposes, autovacuum and transaction ID wraparound; connect a long-lived snapshot to dead tuples.','Hold an old transaction while updating a table repeatedly; observe stats, table size, vacuum and pool waiting.','Release the snapshot and explain what vacuum reclaims versus what it returns to the OS.','Crash/restart a disposable database during writes and inspect recovery logs and acknowledged transaction survival.','Why does increasing the pool sometimes make latency worse?','It adds contention and memory use beyond service capacity. Queueing moves into the database and increases tail latency.')])
course(B,'distributed','Distributed systems and failure semantics','Reason about ordering, consistency, retries and coordination under partial failure.',
'Build a fault-injected replicated key/value simulator with delayed, duplicated and dropped messages. Compare leader reads and stale replica reads; design an idempotent command layer.',
['A deterministic seed reproduces every reported divergence.','Record histories showing stale reads and explain the consistency contract.','A bounded retry policy uses deadlines, backoff and deduplication under ambiguous completion.'],[
L('Partial failure, clocks and consistency','In a distributed system one participant can fail while another continues, and a timeout cannot distinguish a slow peer from a dead peer. Wall clocks can jump and disagree, so timestamp ordering is not automatically causal ordering. Linearizability gives a real-time single-object contract; eventual consistency permits temporary divergence under stated convergence assumptions. A network partition forces a choice about which operations remain available and which consistency guarantees they retain. Specify the contract per operation before selecting a storage product.','raft','Read the paper introduction and safety discussion; distinguish consensus from replication without agreement.','Simulate two replicas with delayed messages and record reads around a leader write.','A history identifies one stale read, its ordering and the promised contract it violates or satisfies.','Add a partition and compare refusing writes with accepting divergent writes.','Does eventual consistency imply a bounded staleness interval?','No. A bound requires additional assumptions and mechanisms; eventual convergence alone does not promise a deadline.'),
L('Retries, idempotency and coordination','Retries amplify work during overload unless bounded by a common deadline and a retry budget. Exponential backoff with jitter spreads retries but does not create capacity. Durable idempotency records require a scope, payload fingerprint, retention policy and atomic relation to the effect. Distributed locks need careful lease and stale-owner reasoning: a paused owner may resume after its lease expires. Fencing tokens let the protected resource reject stale writers instead of trusting timing alone.','design','Read Timeouts, retries, and backoff with jitter and Making retries safe with idempotent APIs.','Build a client that retries transient failures against a deduplicating ledger, including response loss after commit.','Each command changes the ledger once; attempts, elapsed time and retry reasons are visible.','Simulate an expired lease owner resuming and enforce monotonic fencing at the storage boundary.','Why can a lease expire while the old worker still writes?','A pause or partition may prevent renewal without stopping execution. The resource must reject a stale fencing token.')])
course(B,'cache','Caching and performance experiments','Measure the consistency and load consequences of caching rather than adding Redis by reflex.',
'Build a cache-performance experiment for an expensive catalog query: compare no cache, cache-aside, single-flight and stale-while-revalidate under a synchronized expiration burst.',
['Measure hit ratio, origin QPS, p50/p95/p99 and error rate with identical workload seeds.','A hot-key expiration does not produce one origin request per caller.','An update race and a cache outage have explicit, tested freshness and fallback behavior.'],[
L('Cache-aside, invalidation and stale data','A cache stores a derived answer with a freshness contract. Cache-aside reads can race with updates: an old read can repopulate a key after a writer invalidates it. TTL bounds some stale data only if fill and invalidation behavior obey the assumed timing. Negative caching reduces repeated misses but can hide newly created objects. Include tenant and authorization-relevant dimensions in keys, and avoid letting a cache outage turn every request into an unrestricted origin query.','redis','Read invalidation messages, disconnection handling and race conditions; compare server tracking to cache-aside.','Reproduce stale repopulation with barriers around read, update, invalidate and cache fill.','A test deterministically exposes the race; the chosen mitigation states its remaining staleness window.','Add negative caching and tenant scoping, then test object creation and permission revocation.','Does deleting a cache entry after update guarantee freshness?','No. An in-flight read of old state can refill it afterward unless the design addresses that race.'),
L('Stampedes, queueing and measurement','A high hit ratio can coexist with poor tail latency if a hot key expires and thousands of requests rebuild it. Single-flight collapses concurrent work for one key, but each waiter still needs a deadline and memory budget. Stale-while-revalidate exchanges freshness for availability; TTL jitter reduces synchronized expiry across keys. Little’s Law relates average in-flight work, throughput and time in a stable system, but it cannot justify unlimited queues. Compare distributions and origin saturation, not only average response time.','sre','Read Handling Overload and Addressing Cascading Failures; identify where queues form in a cache miss.','Run a fixed-rate load generator through a synchronized expiry with and without single-flight.','The report separates offered load, completed throughput, rejected requests and percentile latency.','Implement stale-while-revalidate with a maximum stale age and a bounded refresh queue.','Why can a cache improve averages while worsening p99?','Rare synchronized misses create bursts and queueing; aggregate averages hide those expensive intervals.')])
course(B,'messaging','Durable jobs, messaging and workflows','Design recoverable work with explicit delivery, ordering and side-effect guarantees.',
'Build a durable job-processing system with PostgreSQL outbox, a broker and independent workers. Include retry scheduling, dead-letter inspection, poison-job handling and restart recovery.',
['Kill a worker after its external effect but before acknowledgement; replay causes no duplicate effect.','A committed business change eventually produces an event after publisher restart.','Poison jobs stop retrying after a bounded policy and can be inspected and replayed deliberately.'],[
L('Acknowledgements, duplicates and the outbox','Publisher confirms tell a producer about broker acceptance; consumer acknowledgements tell the broker about processing progress. Neither alone proves a business side effect occurred exactly once. A worker can commit its effect and die before acknowledging, producing redelivery. The outbox writes a business change and event intent in one database transaction, while a separate publisher may still send duplicates. Consumers therefore need idempotent effects or an inbox deduplication record atomically tied to their local mutation.','rabbit','Read automatic/manual acknowledgement, requeue and publisher confirms; draw the crash windows separately.','Implement an outbox publisher and an inbox-protected account projection; kill each process at every boundary.','No committed event disappears and replayed messages do not double-apply the projection.','Add broker outage recovery without holding business transactions open on publish.','Why does the outbox not remove consumer deduplication?','The publisher can send successfully and crash before recording delivery, so its next attempt may duplicate the event.'),
L('Ordering, retries and long-running jobs','Ordering is usually scoped to a partition or queue, not global. Retrying an earlier message can let later work overtake it, and a poison message can block progress indefinitely. Choose whether the domain needs per-key order, commutative updates or version checks. Long-running jobs require heartbeats, leases or resumable chunks, but lease expiry does not stop old workers. Store progress at safe boundaries and distinguish retryable infrastructure failures from permanent invalid input.','kafka','Read design sections on consumers, replication and delivery semantics; name the scope of each ordering promise.','Implement a job state machine with ready, leased, retry_wait, succeeded and dead states.','A frozen worker cannot finalize a job after lease ownership changes; retries retain attempt history.','Process ordered account events with one missing sequence number and define reconciliation behavior.','When is a dead-letter queue insufficient?','When no operator owns inspection, correction and replay. It is storage, not a recovery procedure.')])
course(B,'reliability','Reliability, observability and incident response','Set service objectives, preserve useful telemetry and practice diagnosis under failure.',
'Run a game day against the reservation/job service. Define an SLO, instrument traces and metrics, inject a slow dependency and produce a blameless incident report with a verified corrective change.',
['An alert detects user-visible budget burn without paging on every transient spike.','A trace links HTTP request, database work and asynchronous job using propagated context.','The runbook diagnoses injected failure within ten minutes using bounded-cardinality telemetry.'],[
L('SLIs, SLOs and overload control','Reliability needs a user-facing definition: a successful request within a latency threshold is more useful than process uptime. An SLO sets a target over a window and an error budget makes tradeoffs explicit. Retries and queues can hide failures while consuming the budget. Shed load before all workers saturate, reserve capacity for essential operations and use circuit breaking only with a clear recovery probe policy. A dependency being down should not make every unrelated endpoint fail.','sre','Read Service Level Objectives, Alerting on SLOs and Handling Overload; define numerator and denominator before choosing a target.','Specify an availability/latency SLI and replay a synthetic hour containing errors, slow successes and rejected requests.','The computed error budget includes the intended user failures and excludes only documented non-service traffic.','Add admission control and graceful degradation for a nonessential recommendation dependency.','Why can queue length be a better early warning than error rate?','Requests may wait long before timing out. Growing queues expose demand exceeding capacity while success counters still look healthy.'),
L('Metrics, traces and useful logs','Metrics summarize populations, traces connect causal work, and logs preserve selected events. A request ID in every metric label creates unbounded cardinality; keep such identifiers in traces or structured logs. Sampling reduces trace cost but can remove rare failure evidence, so document the policy. Propagate context across broker boundaries and record queue wait separately from execution. Logs must be redacted before export, not merely hidden by a dashboard.','otel','Read signals, baggage and context propagation; decide which fields belong in metrics versus traces.','Instrument request latency, queue wait, database duration and tool-like downstream calls with trace context.','One failed job can be followed from request through retry without storing credentials or document bodies.','Introduce missing context and metric-label explosion; detect and repair both in a telemetry review.','Why is baggage a poor place for secrets?','It propagates across service boundaries and may be logged or exported. It is contextual data, not a secure credential channel.')])
course(B,'testing','Testing, profiling and capacity planning','Use properties, fault injection and measurements to challenge implementation assumptions.',
'Build a repeatable verification suite and capacity report for the transactional service. Combine state-machine tests, real PostgreSQL integration tests and fixed-arrival-rate load tests; diagnose the first bottleneck.',
['A seeded concurrent failure reproduces in CI and checks business invariants.','The capacity report states data size, hardware, warmup, workload, percentiles and error rate.','A profiler-backed change improves the measured bottleneck without invalidating correctness tests.'],[
L('Properties, contracts and realistic test boundaries','A mock verifies the interaction you programmed into it, not a database’s isolation or a broker’s redelivery behavior. Use fast unit tests for local logic, contract tests for external shapes and disposable real dependencies for semantics that matter. Property tests explore input spaces, while state-machine tests explore operation sequences and invariants. Deterministic clocks and fault points make recovery tests repeatable; sleeping and hoping for a race makes them flaky.','hypothesis','Read strategies and stateful testing; express inventory conservation as an invariant across reserve/cancel sequences.','Write a state machine that generates reserve, cancel, retry and duplicate commands against a real database.','Shrinking yields a minimal failing sequence for an intentionally introduced double-cancel bug.','Add a provider contract fixture and detect an incompatible error response without calling a live service.','Why does 100% line coverage not prove correct concurrency?','Coverage does not enumerate interleavings, invariants or real dependency behavior; separate semantic tests are required.'),
L('Profiling, load shape and capacity','A profiler attributes observed work; a benchmark determines behavior under a specified workload. CPU profiles miss time spent waiting unless the tool measures it, and a closed-loop load test can reduce offered load exactly when latency rises. Record offered rate, throughput, failures and tail latency independently. Apply one change at a time and retain raw data. Capacity is a boundary under stated resource and reliability constraints, not the largest QPS a process ever briefly reported.','perf','Read profiler output interpretation; contrast cumulative time with self time before selecting a function to optimize.','Profile a serialization-heavy endpoint and compare CPU, allocation and database timing under fixed-rate load.','The report identifies the limiting resource and shows before/after measurements with identical input distributions.','Increase payload size tenfold and test whether the previous optimization still helps.','Why can an apparent p99 improvement be misleading during overload?','A client may stop issuing requests while waiting, hiding queueing and reducing load. Report arrival behavior and rejected or timed-out work.')])
course(B,'deployment','Deployment, infrastructure and architecture','Operate a service through change, recovery and growth with explicit architectural tradeoffs.',
'Production backend capstone: deliver a tenant-aware reservation and durable-job platform with PostgreSQL, cache and broker. Use containers and reproducible infrastructure; include load testing, telemetry, rollback, backup restoration and an architecture review.',
['A clean environment starts from documented commands with no embedded credentials.','Restore a backup into an empty database and verify inventory and job invariants.','During a rolling deployment, in-flight requests drain and mixed-version workers process compatible messages.','Meet a declared SLO at a measured offered load, then demonstrate controlled overload and recovery.','Submit threat model, ADRs, operational runbooks, traces and a cost/capacity estimate.'],[
L('Reproducible builds, releases and recovery','A container packages userspace dependencies; it does not make application state durable or every build reproducible. Pin dependencies, minimize runtime privileges and separate build-time inputs from secrets. Readiness controls traffic eligibility while liveness asks whether restart is useful; conflating them can restart every instance during a shared database outage. Migrations and messages must support overlapping versions. A backup is only evidence of recoverability after a restore drill checks data and recovery time.','docker','Read multi-stage builds, pinning base images and cache; compare build reproducibility with deployment reproducibility.','Create a non-root image, health endpoints and a shutdown sequence; perform a mixed-version rollout.','No secrets appear in image history; draining completes before the declared grace period.','Restore PostgreSQL to an isolated environment and measure recovery time and potential data loss.','Why should liveness avoid depending on a shared database?','A database outage can make every instance restart simultaneously without repairing the dependency, amplifying failure.'),
L('Architecture, system design and engineering decisions','Architecture allocates responsibilities and failure boundaries. A modular monolith often gives simpler transactions and operations than prematurely separated services. Split a service when independent scaling, ownership or isolation justifies network and consistency costs. A system design begins with workload, data access, invariants and reliability goals; boxes follow those constraints. Record alternatives and the evidence that would reverse your choice. Infrastructure as code should make changes reviewable and drift detectable, while managed services exchange control for operational support and cost.','azure','Read the five pillars and reliability design principles; evaluate one design against concrete failure and cost assumptions.','Write an ADR comparing a modular monolith with two services for reservations and billing; estimate peak connections, storage and queue demand.','The ADR states invariants, failure modes, rough capacity arithmetic and a reversible migration path.','Describe a regional outage plan with RPO/RTO and test one recoverable component locally.','What makes an architecture decision stronger than a list of technologies?','It connects requirements to tradeoffs, rejected alternatives and measurable consequences under failure and growth.')],hours=50)
# Optional branches are depth, never prerequisites for the core capstone.
course(B,'consensus','Advanced distributed systems','Study consensus, replication and coordination beyond client-side retry patterns.',
'Implement a deterministic three-node Raft teaching simulator with election, replicated commands and crash/restart. It is a learning artifact, not a production consensus library.',
['A seeded partition/restart trace preserves committed-prefix agreement.','Uncommitted entries cannot be exposed as committed state.','Document omitted production concerns and compare with the paper’s safety argument.'],[
L('Consensus and replicated state machines','Replicating bytes is not enough to agree which commands are committed. Raft separates leader election, log replication and safety rules so a majority can agree despite some failures. Terms distinguish leadership epochs; a stale leader must not continue deciding commits. Consensus assumes a particular failure model and durable state, and does not make arbitrary external side effects deterministic. Use a simulator to inspect histories before attempting networking.','raft','Read sections 5 and 8, Figure 2 and the election restriction; annotate each persistent field.','Simulate an election and a leader crash with an uncommitted suffix.','A new leader preserves committed entries while conflicting uncommitted entries are resolved.','Add duplicate client requests and separate request deduplication from log agreement.','Can consensus make an external email send exactly once?','No. The replicated decision still needs an idempotent or reconcilable external effect.')],True,'distributed',30)
course(B,'database','Database engineering','Explore storage, recovery, replication and operational database design.',
'Build a miniature append-only key/value engine with checksummed records, crash recovery and compaction; compare its guarantees with PostgreSQL WAL and replication.',
['Recovery ignores or rejects a truncated final record without inventing data.','Compaction preserves the latest committed value across restart.','A write-amplification report explains the difference from a production database.'],[
L('Storage engines and recovery contracts','A storage engine must define when an acknowledged write survives a crash. Writing to a userspace buffer, kernel page cache and durable media are different boundaries. Append-only records simplify sequential writes but require indexing and compaction; checksums detect corruption rather than repair it. Replication adds another durability boundary and asynchronous followers can lag. State which failures the design tolerates instead of using durable as an unqualified adjective.','wal','Read WAL reliability and checkpoints; distinguish flushing a log from flushing every data page.','Write length/checksum-framed records and crash during append and compaction.','Restart returns only complete acknowledged records under the documented fsync policy.','Measure batching throughput versus acknowledgement latency and possible loss window.','Why does a replica not replace backups?','Accidental deletes or corruption may replicate. Independent recovery history protects a different failure class.')],True,'postgres',30)
course(B,'platform','Cloud and platform engineering','Turn deployment knowledge into safe automation and multi-service operations.',
'Create a disposable Kubernetes deployment and infrastructure plan for the capstone with resource requests, autoscaling assumptions, secret references and rollback.',
['A rollout with a bad readiness probe stops without taking all healthy replicas offline.','Resource limits and disruption settings have a recorded failure experiment.','The plan includes cleanup, cost bounds and drift detection without committing credentials.'],[
L('Scheduling, autoscaling and platform boundaries','A scheduler places workloads using declared requests; a limit constrains resource usage and may throttle or kill a process. Autoscaling needs a signal that tracks demand and enough startup headroom to react before queues explode. Pod count alone cannot overcome a saturated database. A platform should supply paved paths for configuration, identity, telemetry and recovery while making application-specific responsibilities explicit. Infrastructure changes need preview, review and rollback just like code.','k8s','Read Deployments, Pod lifecycle, resource management and probes; draw rollout and termination timing.','Deploy two replicas, then simulate CPU pressure and a failing readiness check.','Observe scheduling, throttling and traffic removal separately; retain rollout events.','Define an Azure deployment alternative and compare operational responsibility and monthly cost assumptions.','Why may scaling workers reduce total throughput?','Shared dependencies can saturate; more workers increase contention and connection pressure without increasing useful capacity.')],True,'deployment',30)
course(B,'appsec','Security engineering','Go deeper into threat models, abuse resistance and verification of trust boundaries.',
'Conduct an adversarial review of the capstone with a data-flow threat model, an authorization matrix and a prioritized remediation patch.',
['Every trust boundary names an attacker-controlled input and an enforcement point.','At least three reproducible abuse cases become regression tests.','Secret rotation and incident credential revocation are rehearsed on disposable credentials.'],[
L('Threat models and secure operations','A threat model starts with assets, principals, entry points and trust boundaries rather than a generic checklist. Controls must bind to an enforcement point the attacker cannot bypass. SSRF defenses require resolving and validating destinations, controlling redirects and considering rebinding or proxy behavior. Dependency integrity, secret rotation and least-privilege runtime identity reduce blast radius when prevention fails. Security evidence should include negative tests and operational recovery, not only a scanner score.','owasp','Read unsafe consumption of APIs and SSRF, then trace an authenticated user’s data through downstream services.','Draw the capstone data-flow diagram and implement regression tests for cross-tenant access, oversized work and private-network fetches.','Each attack fails at a named boundary and produces redacted diagnostic evidence.','Perform a dependency and container privilege review and remove one unnecessary permission.','Why is input validation alone insufficient for authorization?','Well-formed data can still name an unauthorized resource; identity and policy must be checked for the specific operation.')],True,'security',24)
course(B,'performance','Performance engineering','Study contention, memory locality and efficient I/O with controlled experiments.',
'Build a performance dossier comparing batching, serialization formats and worker/pool sizes for one representative backend workload.',
['Use repeated runs with confidence ranges or variability reporting.','Include peak memory, CPU and tail latency alongside throughput.','A proposed optimization identifies its correctness and operational tradeoffs.'],[
L('Contention, batching and memory costs','Optimization moves costs rather than making them disappear. Batching amortizes per-call overhead while increasing waiting time and failure scope. More concurrency can improve utilization until shared locks, cache lines, allocation or I/O become bottlenecks. Python object overhead and copying can dominate a byte-processing pipeline even when algorithmic complexity looks acceptable. Benchmark representative payload distributions and distinguish a microbenchmark improvement from end-to-end user value.','perf','Read profiler limitations and statistics interpretation; compare wall-clock and CPU accounting.','Measure batch sizes 1, 10 and 100 across serialization plus database insertion.','Report throughput, p99, peak memory and rollback behavior for a failed batch.','Try zero-copy or streaming input and record whether it changes the actual bottleneck.','When is a larger batch the wrong optimization?','When added latency, memory, transaction duration or retry blast radius violates the workload’s constraints.')],True,'testing',24)
course(B,'evolution','Architecture evolution and event sourcing','Explore event-based models only when history and reconstruction justify their complexity.',
'Build a versioned event-sourced order model and replayable projection, then compare it with the core relational implementation.',
['Replay from zero reconstructs the same state as incremental processing.','Old event fixtures remain interpretable after a schema change.','Document consistency lag, repair procedures and why event sourcing may be rejected.'],[
L('Events, projections and semantic versioning','An event records a domain fact that has already happened; a command requests a decision. Event sourcing makes the event log authoritative, so evolution must preserve the meaning of historical records. Projections are replaceable derived views but can lag, duplicate or fail. CQRS can separate read and write models without requiring event sourcing or microservices. Choose it for auditability or reconstruction requirements, not because a broker already exists.','kafka','Read log compaction and consumer offset design; distinguish broker retention from domain history guarantees.','Implement versioned order events and a projection that resumes from a stored offset.','A crash between projection write and checkpoint cannot double-apply the event.','Add a new event version and rebuild a second projection using only historical fixtures.','Why is a queue not automatically an event store?','Retention, ordering scope, replay access and schema guarantees may not meet the domain’s permanent-history requirements.')],True,'messaging',24)

course(I,'foundations','LLM fundamentals and inference','Explain token generation and model limits well enough to debug applications without treating the model as an oracle.',
'Build an inference notebook and CLI using a small local model or a fixture-backed provider adapter. Measure tokenization, sampling variability, context sensitivity and a baseline task set.',
['Record model revision, generation settings, prompt bytes and token usage.','Compare at least 20 fixed tasks across two decoding configurations.','Demonstrate a confident incorrect response and explain why likelihood is not factual confidence.'],[
L('Tokens, attention and next-token prediction','A language model maps a token prefix to a distribution over next tokens. Tokens are encoding units, not words, and different encodings change length and cost. Attention lets a position combine information from accessible positions; it is not a database lookup with guaranteed recall. Training adjusts parameters across examples, while inference uses fixed parameters to generate a continuation. A plausible continuation can be false because likelihood of text is not a verified claim about the world.','transformer','Read embeddings, positional encoding, attention and decoder output; sketch one generation step before studying the paper.','Tokenize English, Greek, JSON and source code; compare character counts with token counts for the actual chosen model.','The artifact records tokenizer identity and identifies a case where characters poorly predict tokens.','Create minimal prompt pairs differing in one relevant fact and inspect response changes.','Does a longer context window guarantee use of every supplied fact?','No. Capacity limits accepted input; reliable use depends on model behavior, position and task. Evaluate evidence retrieval separately.'),
L('Decoding, inference cost and uncertainty','Prefill processes the input context; decoding generates output incrementally and often has different performance characteristics. Temperature changes distribution sharpness and top-p restricts candidate mass, but neither is a calibrated confidence score. Greedy generation can still vary across runtimes and model revisions. KV caching avoids recomputing some attention work while consuming memory proportional to active sequences and context. Compare task success, latency and cost under fixed workloads before choosing settings.','hf','Read greedy decoding and multinomial sampling; identify which options the selected model actually supports.','Run a fixed extraction task 20 times at two supported sampling settings and measure validity and exact-match success.','Report failures, token usage, time to first token and full completion latency separately.','Increase context length while holding output size fixed and explain the latency/memory trend.','Why can temperature zero still fail repeatability across deployments?','Model revisions, kernels, batching and numerical differences can change outputs. Reproducibility requires pinned artifacts and recorded environment.')])
course(I,'interfaces','Model APIs, structured output and streaming','Create a typed provider boundary that handles partial responses and failure honestly.',
'Build a provider-independent Python completion interface with one real provider adapter and a scripted fake. Support text, tool calls, usage, stop reasons and streaming events.',
['Malformed output, refusal, rate limit and truncated stream have distinct typed outcomes.','A stream can be cancelled without leaving an open response.','Contract tests run offline and a separately marked live smoke test records the model identifier.'],[
L('Typed requests and validated results','A provider response is a protocol object containing content, termination information and usage, not just a string. Structured output constrains shape but cannot ensure that values are correct or authorized. Refusals, length limits and incomplete tool arguments must remain visible to the caller. Normalize providers into a small internal model without discarding information needed for recovery, and validate untrusted JSON at the boundary before executing anything derived from it.','api','Read request/response examples, stop_reason and usage; map each into an internal typed event or result.','Implement a fake and one provider adapter for a typed extraction request.','Fixtures cover text success, tool request, refusal, length stop, invalid JSON and missing required fields.','Add semantic validation for dates and amounts after schema validation.','Can schema-valid output authorize a payment?','No. Schema validity checks structure; application policy and user authority must independently approve the effect.'),
L('Streaming is an event protocol','A text stream is not a sequence of complete messages, and a tool-argument delta is not independently valid JSON. Accumulate fragments by the provider’s block or call identifiers and validate only at the appropriate completion boundary. Errors can occur after a successful HTTP status and after visible output. Distinguish provisional display from committed conversation history; cancellation should close the transport and mark the partial turn rather than silently saving it as complete.','stream','Read event order, content block deltas and errors during streaming; trace one tool-use stream.','Parse a recorded event stream split across arbitrary byte boundaries, including partial JSON tool arguments.','No tool executes before its complete validated arguments; a midstream error preserves an incomplete status.','Add client cancellation and a maximum buffered tool-argument size.','Why should a UI not treat first text as final success?','Generation can later stop with an error, truncation or tool request; visible tokens are provisional until termination is interpreted.')])
course(I,'tools','Tool calling and dispatch from primitives','Build a controlled bridge from model proposals to deterministic application operations.',
'Implement a typed tool registry and dispatcher for calculator, document search and a mock write operation. Run an adversarial fixture suite without relying on a model.',
['Unknown names, malformed inputs and oversized results cannot reach tool implementation.','Read-only tools can run concurrently with stable result-to-call association.','Write operations pass an independent authorization gate and return bounded, redacted results.'],[
L('Schemas are contracts, tool calls are proposals','The model selects a tool name and arguments, but application code owns discovery, validation, authorization and execution. Tool descriptions influence selection and are not trusted policy. A registry should map names to schemas, implementation and explicit effects rather than executing arbitrary names through eval. Preserve call IDs so concurrent results are associated with the correct request. Validate outputs as well as inputs, and separate an ordinary tool failure from a broken transport or harness bug.','schema','Read required, additionalProperties and composition; design a closed schema for a mock transfer request.','Create a registry with typed arguments, explicit result envelopes and no dynamic code evaluation.','Unknown tools and invalid argument fields fail before side effects; every result retains its call ID.','Fuzz nested and oversized arguments and apply depth/size limits before expensive processing.','Why can a tool named read_file still be dangerous?','Names and annotations are claims, not enforcement. The implementation and permissions determine accessible paths and effects.'),
L('Dispatch, permissions and execution budgets','Tool execution crosses a trust boundary and consumes finite time, output space and external authority. Give each call a deadline, concurrency limit and result-size budget. Parallelize independent reads, but serialize dependent operations and writes unless the domain supports safe concurrency. A retry policy must classify side effects: a timed-out write may have completed. A permission decision should bind to normalized arguments and the current principal so it cannot be reused for a different action.','async','Read TaskGroup and timeout behavior; define cancellation ownership between a run and a tool task.','Dispatch three independent read tools with per-call budgets and a policy-gated mock write.','One timed-out read does not mislabel another result; denied writes never invoke the implementation.','Simulate a write that commits then times out and require reconciliation rather than blind retry.','Why does validating arguments twice help?','Normalize and validate before policy evaluation, then ensure the executed arguments are exactly those authorized; avoid mutation or substitution between stages.')])
course(I,'loop','Agent execution loops and context management','Implement the loop yourself and make each transition inspectable.',
'Build a framework-independent agent loop over the provider and dispatcher interfaces. Support multiple tool rounds, explicit terminal states, bounded context and a replayable fake model.',
['Fixtures cover text termination, multi-tool rounds, tool errors and repeated identical calls.','The loop terminates on step, elapsed-time, token or cost budget exhaustion.','Tool-call/result pairs remain intact during context trimming.'],[
L('The agent is a state machine','An agent harness repeatedly prepares context, asks a model for a next action, validates the response, executes permitted tools and appends observations. The model does not own the loop. Represent states such as ready, awaiting_model, awaiting_tools, awaiting_approval, completed and failed explicitly. Stop reasons and budgets determine transitions; a nonempty string alone is not a termination signal. Persist enough evidence to explain why each action happened and test the loop with scripted model responses before live calls.','json','Read request identifiers and error objects, then define internal events that are independent of any provider protocol.','Implement the state machine with a fake model returning two tool rounds followed by an answer.','A transition log accounts for every call, result, error and terminal reason with deterministic fixture replay.','Inject malformed model actions and a repeating tool cycle; stop with typed errors and bounded attempts.','What turns a chatbot with tools into an agent loop?','Application-controlled repeated action/observation transitions with state and termination policy, rather than a single completion.'),
L('Context is a budgeted working set','Context should contain the minimum reliable evidence needed for the next decision: instructions, current goal, relevant history and tool observations. Trimming arbitrary messages can orphan tool results or delete user constraints. Summaries are lossy derived data and should retain provenance to original events. Reserve output and tool-result headroom before calling the model, and estimate with the actual tokenizer when available. Separate durable event history from the selected prompt view so compression never destroys the audit trail.','hf','Read generation max_new_tokens and input length behavior; inspect the chosen provider’s context accounting rules.','Implement a context selector that keeps instructions, recent complete turns and tool-call/result groups within a token budget.','A small-context test never leaves orphan results and always retains the latest user goal.','Add a summary with source event IDs and compare task success before and after compression.','Why should the event log be larger than the prompt?','The log preserves evidence and replay; the prompt is a bounded, derived working set optimized for the next inference.')])
course(I,'mcp','MCP architecture and versioned interoperability','Learn the actual protocol boundary and integrate tools without confusing MCP with model tool calling.',
'Add an MCP adapter to the harness using a supported official SDK revision or a small explicitly limited client. Integrate a disposable local server and a recorded HTTP server fixture.',
['Declare supported protocol revisions and retain compatibility transcripts for each.','List tools across pagination and map MCP schemas/results without losing error distinctions.','Cancellation closes or cancels the request according to the selected transport and revision.','No legacy initialize/session assumptions are silently applied to the 2026-07-28 baseline.'],[
L('Hosts, clients, servers and protocol revisions','The host owns model interaction and user policy; an MCP client communicates with a server exposing tools, resources or prompts. MCP is not the model provider’s tool-call protocol: an adapter must map between them. The 2026-07-28 baseline uses per-request protocol and capability metadata and server/discover capability discovery, whereas older revisions used a connection-scoped initialize handshake. Result types and compatibility behavior also differ. Pin the intended revision and SDK support explicitly; a current specification and an installed SDK may target different protocol eras.','mcpbase','Read messages, request metadata, resultType and version compatibility alongside the transport overview. Compare with an older revision only in a labeled compatibility fixture.','Create transcripts for tools/list and tools/call under the pinned revision and validate metadata and IDs.','Tests distinguish a protocol error, a tool execution error and an unsupported revision.','Add an older supported revision behind an explicit adapter rather than mixing wire shapes.','Why is MCP discovery not permission to execute a tool?','Discovery describes available capabilities; host policy must still authorize the specific operation and arguments.'),
L('Transport, tool discovery and authentication','stdio frames messages over a launched subprocess and must keep diagnostics off the protocol stream. Streamable HTTP binds messages to POST requests and may stream responses; framing and cancellation differ from stdio. In the 2026-07-28 baseline, per-request metadata is authoritative and HTTP mirrors selected fields in headers. Tool lists can be paginated and vary with request authorization. HTTP authorization uses protected-resource and authorization-server discovery; do not forward arbitrary upstream tokens to a server or assume stdio follows the same OAuth flow.','mcptransport','Read both transport binding links, request metadata, cancellation and backward compatibility. Then read pinned tools and authorization pages in the additional resources.','Launch a local MCP server, enumerate all tool pages and invoke a bounded read tool through the harness adapter.','stderr logs cannot corrupt framing; a broken subprocess and a cancelled request leave no hanging run.','Mock expired HTTP credentials and metadata/header mismatch; verify failure without leaking tokens.','Can a named-pipe bridge call itself standard MCP transport automatically?','No. A custom transport must document framing and preserve the protocol’s message and metadata rules; local IPC alone does not establish conformance.')])
course(I,'durability','Durable state and recoverable orchestration','Make runs resumable without duplicating external actions.',
'Persist runs, events, tool intents and outcomes in SQLite or PostgreSQL. Resume an interrupted agent execution and support a manual approval state.',
['Crash injection at every persistence boundary yields an explicit recoverable or uncertain state.','A committed mock write is not repeated after restart.','Pending approval resumes only for the same principal, tool and normalized arguments.'],[
L('Event history, checkpoints and replay','A checkpoint records sufficient state to continue a run; an event log explains how it got there. Persist model decisions and tool intents before executing effects, then record outcomes separately. A crash between external completion and local recording leaves uncertainty that local transactions cannot erase. Replay should use recorded model/tool outputs by default, not silently call live services again. Version stored schemas and retain migration tests for old runs.','sqlite','Read transaction modes and rollback behavior; draw durable boundaries for model response, intent, effect and result.','Build append-only run events plus a projected current-state table in one transaction.','After restart, the projection reconstructs the same terminal state from events.','Inject a crash after a write and require deduplication or an operator reconciliation record.','Why does saving after every step not guarantee exactly-once effects?','The external effect and local checkpoint are separate commits; a crash between them leaves an ambiguous outcome.'),
L('Workflows before open-ended autonomy','A deterministic workflow makes known transitions explicit; an agent delegates selected decisions to a model. Use workflows for stable business rules and agents where the action sequence depends on evolving evidence. Durable orchestration adds retries, waiting states and human input, but framework persistence does not automatically make tool side effects replay-safe. After implementing your state machine, inspect a framework’s checkpoint and replay rules and map them to the same failure windows.','langgraph','Read threads, checkpoints, pending writes and replay; compare each to your event-log design.','Implement an approval wait and a two-stage retrieve/verify workflow without a framework.','Waiting consumes no worker loop; restart preserves the pending action and approval identity.','Port only the orchestration layer to LangGraph and compare replay behavior with the primitive implementation.','When should an agent decision become deterministic code?','When the rule is stable, testable and required for correctness or policy; keep probabilistic choice outside that enforcement boundary.')])
course(I,'retrieval','Retrieval and grounded answers','Build a measurable search pipeline before adding agentic retrieval complexity.',
'Build a document retrieval service with lexical and vector baselines, provenance-bearing chunks and a held-out query set. Integrate it as a harness tool.',
['Evaluate at least 30 queries with relevance labels and report recall@k and ranking quality.','Every returned chunk records document ID, version, offsets and access scope.','Cross-tenant queries and deleted documents never appear in results.'],[
L('Indexing, chunking and retrieval evaluation','Retrieval selects evidence from a corpus; generation synthesizes an answer from that evidence. Chunk boundaries trade local relevance against missing context, and embeddings encode similarity rather than truth. Begin with lexical search as a baseline, then compare dense and hybrid retrieval using labeled queries. Keep document versions and offsets so evidence can be inspected. Access filters belong inside retrieval, not only in the final prompt, because leaked chunks already cross the boundary.','ir','Read ranked retrieval and evaluation chapters; define relevance judgments and recall@k before choosing embeddings.','Index a small technical corpus with heading-aware chunks and build a 30-query relevance set.','The report includes lexical baseline recall@5 and at least five inspected failure cases.','Compare fixed-size chunks with section chunks using the same queries and corpus version.','Why is answer quality insufficient to evaluate retrieval?','A model can answer from prior knowledge or guess. Retrieval needs its own evidence-selection metrics and labels.'),
L('Hybrid search, reranking and grounded generation','Lexical retrieval preserves exact names while dense retrieval helps with semantic paraphrases. Hybrid ranking combines candidate lists; a reranker can improve precision at additional latency and cost. Approximate vector indexes trade recall for efficiency and filtering can change observed behavior. Return compact evidence with provenance and require the answer to distinguish supported claims from uncertainty. A citation is useful only if it points to an accessible source passage that supports the nearby claim.','azuresearch','Read hybrid retrieval and reciprocal rank fusion; compare with pgvector exact versus approximate search and filtering.','Compare lexical, dense and hybrid top-k results, then add a reranking stage with a fixed candidate budget.','Report retrieval quality and latency by stage; every generated citation resolves to a supporting indexed passage.','Create an unanswerable query set and require abstention when evidence is insufficient.','Can a reranker recover a document missing from all candidate lists?','No. Candidate recall is an upstream ceiling; reranking only reorders what it receives.')])
course(I,'memory','Memory and context lifecycle','Separate searchable memory from raw artifacts and enforce retention and provenance.',
'Add an artifact-backed memory service to the harness: metadata and searchable text in a database, binary payloads behind an explicit fetch operation. Implement deletion and provenance.',
['Search results do not silently hydrate large binaries into model context.','A deleted record and its derived chunks become unavailable to subsequent retrieval.','Memory writes preserve origin, timestamp, scope and confidence or verification status.'],[
L('History, working memory and long-term records','Conversation history is evidence of prior interaction, not automatically a set of durable facts. Working memory is the selected context for one decision, while long-term memory stores reusable records with provenance and lifecycle rules. Model-generated summaries can be wrong and should not silently become authoritative user preferences. Separate an observation from an inference and record source event IDs. A memory write policy should decide what deserves retention, who may read it and when it expires.','ir','Read classification and retrieval evaluation sections; define a memory query set separately from document search.','Implement typed memory records for observation, preference and derived summary with provenance and scope.','A corrected preference supersedes the old record without losing the audit relation.','Add TTL and user deletion, then test search and prompt construction after expiry.','Why should every model response not become permanent memory?','It creates noise, reinforces hallucinations and retains unnecessary private data. Retention should have purpose and verification rules.'),
L('Artifacts, hydration and consistency','A searchable artifact record can contain a caption, extracted text, MIME type, checksum and storage locator while leaving raw bytes outside prompt history. Retrieval returns descriptors; an explicit hydration step fetches bytes only when needed and within a size budget. Database metadata and blob storage do not share a transaction, so use staged creation and reconciliation for orphaned or missing blobs. Keep access checks at both search and hydration to prevent a previously discovered locator becoming a permanent capability.','pgvector','Read storing vectors with relational metadata and filtering; design an artifact table with checksum, state and access scope.','Store image/file bytes separately from searchable descriptors and expose search_memory and fetch_artifact tools.','Search does not fetch bytes; hydration rejects revoked access and oversized content.','Crash between blob upload and metadata commit and implement an orphan reconciliation job.','Why recheck permissions during hydration?','Access may have changed since search, and a locator alone should not authorize raw-data retrieval.')])
course(I,'evaluation','Evaluation and regression engineering','Measure task success and failure modes with repeatable datasets rather than impressive demos.',
'Create an offline harness evaluation runner with scripted tools, held-out tasks, budget scoring and adversarial cases. Compare two prompt or context policies.',
['At least 40 tasks include normal, unanswerable, tool-failure and malicious-input cases.','Reports separate task success, policy violations, cost, latency and tool correctness.','Any model-judged score is calibrated against a human-labeled subset and does not replace deterministic checks.'],[
L('Datasets, scorers and experimental discipline','An evaluation defines tasks, environment, allowed actions and a scoring procedure. Separate development tasks from a held-out set so prompt iteration does not overfit the benchmark. Deterministic assertions can verify final state and forbidden effects more reliably than prose similarity. A model judge can assess nuanced answers but introduces bias, variance and prompt-injection risk. Record dataset, model and harness versions and inspect failures by category rather than reporting one average score.','evals','Read datasets, solvers, scorers and logs; map your harness into these roles without adopting the framework yet.','Create JSONL tasks with expected tool effects, answer evidence and policy constraints.','The runner exports per-task artifacts and detects a deliberately introduced cross-tenant tool bug.','Blind-label ten outputs and compare human judgments with a model judge’s decisions.','Why can a higher average score be a regression?','A critical subgroup or safety invariant can worsen while easy cases improve. Report category outcomes and hard violation counts.'),
L('Stochasticity, replay and adversarial regression','Live evaluations measure a model/system combination; replay evaluations isolate application logic using recorded decisions. Both are useful but answer different questions. Repeated trials estimate variability and should report uncertainty, not imply a single run proves superiority. Tool environments need resettable state and deterministic failure injection. Adversarial tasks should test unauthorized actions and misleading tool output, not merely ask the model to say it is safe.','evals','Read task state, epochs and evaluation logs; distinguish rerunning inference from replaying a stored trace.','Run baseline and candidate policies on identical task fixtures with multiple trials and fixed budgets.','Report per-category deltas, variability and all unauthorized-effect failures.','Turn the three worst failures into minimized regression fixtures that run without network access.','When is replay insufficient to approve a prompt change?','Recorded decisions bypass the changed model interaction. Live or newly generated evaluations are needed to measure the new policy.')])
course(I,'security','Permissions, prompt injection and sandboxing','Enforce authority in code and contain untrusted tool inputs and outputs.',
'Add a permission engine, approval records and a sandboxed execution boundary to the harness. Run an attack suite using malicious retrieved documents and tool descriptions.',
['Retrieved instructions cannot grant a tool permission or change an approved argument.','Denied network, file and subprocess operations fail at an enforcement boundary.','Credentials are absent from prompts, exported traces and sandbox environments unless explicitly required.'],[
L('Prompt injection and the confused deputy','A model can be influenced by instructions embedded in retrieved text, tool results or repository files. Labeling data as untrusted helps interpretation but is not an enforcement boundary. The harness must check principal, resource, operation and normalized arguments independently of model intent. Tool annotations and descriptions are untrusted claims. Approval must bind to an exact effect and expire or invalidate when arguments, identity or relevant policy changes.','agentsec','Read prompt injection and excessive agency; trace an attack from a document to an attempted privileged tool call.','Inject a document asking the agent to export secrets and call a write tool outside the requested scope.','The model may propose the action, but policy blocks it and records a redacted reason before execution.','Add a time-of-check/time-of-use test that mutates arguments after approval.','Why is a system prompt not a permission system?','It influences model behavior but cannot guarantee enforcement. Privileged effects must be controlled by deterministic code.'),
L('Sandbox boundaries and credential scope','A sandbox constrains effects even if generated code behaves maliciously. A subprocess is not a sandbox by itself, and filesystem isolation does not prevent network exfiltration. Combine resource limits, restricted filesystem, process isolation and outbound network policy appropriate to the threat model. Keep provider and service credentials outside generated-code environments. Audit what remains shared, including mounts, sockets, kernel surface and inherited environment, and use disposable test data when validating containment.','sandbox','Read seccomp filtering, limitations and privilege requirements; identify what it does not isolate.','Run an untrusted script under a documented container or OS sandbox with denied network and a writable scratch directory only.','Tests attempt parent-path reads, network egress, fork growth and timeout escape; each has an observed enforced result.','Add per-tool scoped credentials through a broker rather than the sandbox environment.','Does seccomp alone isolate the filesystem?','No. It filters system calls; filesystem visibility, credentials, mounts and network policy need separate controls.')])
course(I,'operations','Latency, observability and production reliability','Operate agent runs as budgeted distributed workloads with partial failures.',
'Instrument the harness and run a dependency-failure game day. Compare serial and safe parallel tool scheduling, context policies and model-routing decisions.',
['A trace explains model time, tool time, queueing, retries, token usage and terminal reason.','A provider outage does not create an unbounded retry or spending loop.','Prompt and trace redaction is tested before telemetry leaves the process.'],[
L('Tracing runs and controlling cost','An agent run spans model calls, tool execution, queue waits and user approvals. Trace these as distinct spans linked by stable run and call IDs. Record model revision, token usage, cache information when available and outcome categories, while redacting private content. Cost budgets require a conservative estimate before a call and reconciliation afterward; providers may return usage late or incompletely on interruption. Do not let a dashboard’s success label hide a budget stop or a denied action.','otel','Read traces, span context and sampling; define an agent span vocabulary without putting run IDs in metric labels.','Emit a trace for a multi-round run with one failed tool and one retry.','The trace accounts for wall time and estimated/actual usage and contains no fixture secret.','Add a canary secret and verify redaction across logs, spans and persisted evaluation artifacts.','Why is tokens per request insufficient for agent cost?','One user request can create many model rounds, tools and retries. Cost must be aggregated at run and workload levels.'),
L('Latency budgets, routing and graceful failure','Time to first token matters for interaction, but total completion time includes tool rounds and queued work. Parallel independent reads can reduce latency; parallel writes can violate causality or increase blast radius. Routing easy tasks to cheaper models needs evaluation of failure and escalation rates, not just token prices. Propagate deadlines, bound retries and distinguish a resumable interruption from a completed answer. A fallback model may require different tool schema and context capabilities, so fallback is a tested adapter path.','design','Read timeout and retry articles; allocate a run budget across provider, tools, queueing and cleanup.','Simulate provider rate limits, delayed tools and missing usage; enforce a run deadline and maximum spend.','No run exceeds the configured step limit; uncertain cost is surfaced and retries share the original deadline.','Compare a cheap-first router with one strong model on the same held-out task set.','When can a cheap model cost more overall?','Lower per-call price can be outweighed by extra rounds, retries, longer outputs or expensive escalations.')])
course(I,'capstone','Independent agent harness capstone','Integrate primitives into a reliable Python harness and compare it with established implementations.',
'Deliver a framework-independent Python agent harness for a technical-document assistant. Include typed model adapters, streaming, tool dispatch, MCP integration, durable event state, retrieval, artifact memory, policy enforcement, budgets, traces and repeatable evaluations. Do not use an orchestration framework inside the core loop.',
['A clean checkout runs offline fixture evaluations with one documented command.','At least 40 held-out tasks include tool failures, denial, malicious evidence and unanswerable questions.','Crash/restart preserves completed effects and surfaces ambiguous writes for reconciliation.','Integrate one real MCP server with declared supported revisions and no embedded credentials.','Provide a budget/latency report, threat model, architecture decisions, runbook and trace examples.','Compare one framework implementation against the primitive harness and explain replay, context and permission differences.'],[
L('Designing the harness boundaries','Keep the model provider, context selector, tool registry, policy engine, persistence and scheduler behind small interfaces with explicit ownership. The core loop should depend on those contracts rather than a provider’s raw JSON or an SDK’s global client. Pure transition functions and scripted fakes make failures reproducible. Durable events should describe domain actions rather than incidental Python objects so stored runs survive refactoring. Favor a narrow, understandable harness over a general plugin system without a concrete use case.','mcpsdk','Inspect client examples and supported revisions; identify the adapter boundary rather than copying SDK internals into the loop.','Write interface contracts and a vertical slice from user input through model/tool round to persisted answer.','The same run executes using fake providers/tools without network and real adapters without changing loop code.','Replace one persistence or provider adapter and rerun all contract tests.','Which code must never be delegated to the model?','Authorization, validation, resource limits, durable transition rules and enforcement of side-effect policy remain deterministic application responsibilities.'),
L('Release review and framework comparison','A harness is ready for a bounded use case when its guarantees, unsupported behaviors and operating limits are explicit and verified. Review failure recovery and policy boundaries before polishing a demo. Frameworks can supply useful scheduling and persistence, but adopting one transfers assumptions that must be understood. Compare the same workflow and failure fixtures in your harness and one established framework, then decide which abstraction reduces work without obscuring critical behavior. Keep protocol and model dependency updates behind regression evaluations.','langgraph','Revisit persistence and fault-tolerance documentation; map each guarantee to a passing fixture or an explicit limitation.','Perform a release review using the full capstone criteria, then port a small workflow to LangGraph for comparison.','The review links each claim to a test, trace or reproducible command and lists unsupported cases honestly.','Have another engineer follow the runbook or perform a cold-start self-review without relying on shell history.','What evidence would justify replacing the custom loop with a framework?','Equivalent or stronger tested semantics for the target workload, lower maintenance burden and visible policy/replay behavior, rather than feature count alone.')],hours=60)
course(I,'multiagent','Multi-agent systems','Evaluate whether decomposition improves a task enough to justify coordination overhead.',
'Build a bounded supervisor/worker experiment comparing one agent, a deterministic workflow and two specialized agents on the same tasks.',
['Compare at least 20 held-out tasks for success, cost, latency and disagreement.','Workers cannot recursively create unbounded workers or inherit broader permissions.','A failed worker leaves an inspectable partial result and a bounded recovery path.'],[
L('Coordination, handoffs and shared state','Multiple agents add communication and state-management problems as well as additional reasoning opportunities. Roles alone do not create independent evidence when all agents share the same misleading context. Define ownership of subtasks, message schemas, completion conditions and shared artifacts. Bound delegation depth and aggregate budgets at the root run. Prefer a deterministic fan-out when tasks are known and independent; use dynamic delegation only when the task requires it and evaluation shows benefit.','evals','Read multi-agent evaluation examples if available; otherwise map multiple solvers to a single task state and scoring contract.','Implement two workers producing evidence-bearing reports and a supervisor that resolves disagreement.','Every claim retains source provenance; budget accounting includes all child runs.','Add a malicious worker message and verify it cannot expand permissions or overwrite the root goal.','Why can agreement between agents be weak evidence?','They may share model biases, prompts and sources; correlated errors are not independent confirmation.')],True,'evaluation',24)
course(I,'ragdeep','Advanced RAG and memory','Explore retrieval planning, temporal memory and ablation-driven improvements.',
'Compare query rewriting, hybrid retrieval, reranking and memory policies through controlled ablations on a versioned corpus.',
['Each stage has a quality/latency/cost delta against the same baseline.','Temporal queries resolve the correct document version.','Deleted or revoked records disappear from both candidates and hydrated evidence.'],[
L('Retrieval planning and temporal truth','Query rewriting can improve recall while drifting from the user’s intent. Multi-hop retrieval adds another opportunity for unsupported intermediate conclusions. Temporal memory must distinguish when a fact was valid from when it was recorded, and updates need explicit supersession rather than accumulating contradictory snippets. Evaluate each added stage independently so a sophisticated pipeline does not hide a weak candidate set. Use provenance and held-out temporal queries to detect stale or invented evidence.','pgvector','Read exact/approximate filtering and index tuning; design a temporal relevance dataset before tuning.','Create documents with revisions and evaluate current-state and historical questions separately.','Ablation results show whether rewriting, reranking and memory each help their intended query category.','Implement a two-hop query planner with a fixed retrieval budget and evidence for each hop.','Why can more retrieved context reduce quality?','It can dilute relevant evidence, introduce contradictions, increase cost and create additional injection surface.')],True,'memory',30)
course(I,'local','Local models and inference systems','Understand serving, quantization and hardware tradeoffs without assuming local means free.',
'Serve a local model behind the harness provider interface and compare quantization, batching and context sizes under a fixed task workload.',
['Record model license, revision, quantization, hardware, memory and serving configuration.','Measure time to first token, throughput and task success under concurrent requests.','OOM and overload fail predictably without corrupting harness state.'],[
L('Quantization, batching and KV memory','Quantization reduces representation precision to save memory and bandwidth, but task quality can change and not every layer or runtime behaves identically. Continuous batching improves hardware utilization while scheduling requests with different lengths. KV cache memory grows with active sequence context, so long requests can reduce concurrency even when model weights fit. A local server still needs admission control, licensing review, telemetry and a security boundary. Compare total operating cost and latency with hosted inference for the actual workload.','vllm','Read serving configuration, memory utilization and quantization support; compare with llama.cpp supported formats.','Run one model at two quantization levels and two context sizes using identical task prompts.','The report includes task accuracy, memory peak, TTFT and throughput at concurrency 1 and 4.','Add an admission limit and reject oversized contexts before allocation.','Why does fitting model weights in VRAM not guarantee serving capacity?','KV cache, activations, runtime buffers and concurrent sequences also consume memory.')],True,'operations',30)
course(I,'multimodal','Multimodal agents','Add images and other artifacts while preserving ordering, provenance and bounded hydration.',
'Build an image-aware inspection assistant that retrieves artifact descriptors, explicitly hydrates selected images and answers with source references.',
['Image order and source identity survive persistence and replay.','Corrupt, oversized and unsupported media fail before model submission.','Evaluation includes unreadable images and visually ambiguous cases requiring abstention.'],[
L('Media content blocks and evidence','Multimodal messages contain ordered content parts whose meaning depends on the provider’s schema and preprocessing. An image is not a reliable substitute for exact structured data: small text, spatial judgments and ambiguous scenes can fail. Keep original bytes and provenance separate from resized inference representations. Validate MIME type, decoded dimensions and byte size, and make hydration explicit so search does not flood context with media. Never infer a capability or limit from a different model’s documentation.','vision','Read image content blocks, supported formats, size limits and limitations for the exact selected model.','Persist a message containing text, two images and a follow-up question, then replay it through the provider adapter.','Replayed content preserves order and source references; oversized and corrupt image fixtures are rejected.','Compare original and downscaled images on a labeled small-text task and report errors.','Why must image preprocessing be versioned in evaluations?','Resizing, cropping and encoding alter the evidence the model sees and can change accuracy independently of the prompt.')],True,'memory',24)
course(I,'sandboxing','Agent security and isolation engineering','Test containment against concrete generated-code and tool abuse scenarios.',
'Build a disposable execution service with filesystem, process, CPU, memory and network restrictions plus per-request audit records.',
['An adversarial fixture suite cannot access host secrets or unrestricted network.','Timeout cleanup removes descendant processes and temporary artifacts.','The threat model states kernel and infrastructure trust assumptions.'],[
L('Isolation as defense in depth','No single isolation mechanism covers every threat. Containers share a kernel; language-level restrictions are often bypassable; network allowlists must consider DNS, redirects and proxies. Treat execution as hostile and minimize inherited descriptors, environment and mounts. Limits on output and process descendants matter as much as a wall-clock timeout. Design the broker so privileged credentials are never available to arbitrary code and validate returned artifacts before consuming them.','sandbox','Read seccomp restrictions and caveats, then map filesystem and network isolation to separate mechanisms.','Run fork, output-growth, path-escape and egress attempts in a disposable restricted runner.','Each attempt has a recorded enforced denial or a documented unsupported boundary; cleanup leaves no descendants.','Add a narrowly scoped credential broker and demonstrate that code cannot request a different resource.','Why is a timeout around subprocess.wait insufficient?','It may stop waiting without killing descendants, closing descriptors or cleaning resources; lifecycle enforcement must cover the process tree.')],True,'security',30)
course(I,'models','Model engineering and adaptation','Learn when fine-tuning is justified and how to evaluate it against prompting and retrieval.',
'Run a small adapter-training experiment or a fully specified low-cost reproducible training configuration with a real small-model trial. Compare with a prompt-only baseline.',
['Training, validation and held-out data are split by source/task to reduce leakage.','A real training run records model revision, seed, configuration and resource use.','Report quality, safety regressions and whether adaptation is worth maintaining.'],[
L('Training objectives, LoRA and data quality','Pretraining learns broad next-token structure while supervised adaptation changes behavior on a narrower distribution. LoRA trains low-rank updates rather than all model weights, reducing trainable state but not removing data and evaluation requirements. Fine-tuning is often appropriate for stable style or task behavior, while frequently changing knowledge belongs in retrieval. Deduplicate and split data by source so near-identical examples do not inflate held-out scores. A lower training loss does not establish better user outcomes.','peft','Read conceptual guides on adapters and LoRA; identify trainable parameters and inference merge behavior.','Train a small LoRA adapter on a narrow structured-output task with a licensed dataset and a source-level split.','Compare prompt-only and adapted model performance on held-out tasks and report malformed outputs.','Introduce a distribution-shift test and inspect whether adaptation worsens unrelated tasks.','Why can validation accuracy be misleading after random row splitting?','Near-duplicate or source-correlated examples can leak across splits, making memorization look like generalization.')],True,'evaluation',40)
course(I,'evalsystems','Evaluation infrastructure','Build scalable, reviewable evaluation operations and protect benchmark integrity.',
'Create an evaluation service or CLI pipeline with dataset versioning, isolated task state, parallel workers, result comparison and reviewable reports.',
['Interrupted runs resume without silently mixing candidate versions.','Worker concurrency preserves task isolation and root budgets.','Reports include per-task evidence and uncertainty rather than only a leaderboard.'],[
L('Evaluation as a production data pipeline','Evaluation infrastructure must version inputs, reset environments, attribute results and preserve enough artifacts for review. Parallel execution changes rate limits and can contaminate state if tasks share mutable resources. Store run metadata immutably and separate scorer versions from model versions. Statistical uncertainty and subgroup regressions should be visible in comparisons. Protect held-out data from accidental training or prompt-development leakage, and track human overrides as review records rather than silently editing scores.','evals','Read parallelism, sandboxing and log structure; define immutable identifiers for dataset, solver and scorer.','Implement resumable evaluation batches with isolated task stores and deterministic result identifiers.','Restart does not duplicate completed task effects; candidate comparisons use identical dataset/scorer versions.','Bootstrap paired score differences and display confidence ranges alongside critical failure counts.','Why should scorer changes trigger a new report version?','They change the measurement instrument; old and new scores are not directly comparable unless both candidates are rescored.')],True,'evaluation',30)

def compile_track(track,source):
 C=[]; LS=[]; A=[]; R=[]; used=set(); previous=None
 core=[x for x in source if not x['optional']]; optional=[x for x in source if x['optional']]
 ids={x['slug']:f"{track}-{x['slug']}" for x in source}
 for n,c in enumerate(core+optional,1):
  cid=ids[c['slug']]; dep=ids[c['dep']] if c['dep'] else previous
  course_lessons=[]
  for j,row in enumerate(c['lessons'],1):
   title,concept,r,focus,lab,evidence,challenge,q,answer=row; lid=f'{cid}-l{j:02}';course_lessons.append(lid);used.add(r)
   aids=[lid+'-lab',lid+'-challenge']
   LS.append(dict(id=lid,course_id=cid,ordinal=j,type='lesson',title=title,explanation=concept.split('. ')[0]+'.',concepts_in_context=concept,why_it_matters=c['summary'],outcomes=[lab,evidence,challenge],reading=[dict(resource_id=f'{track}-res-{r}',priority='required',reading_focus=focus,estimated_minutes=45)],assignments=aids,knowledge_check=dict(question=q,self_review='Answer before revealing guidance. Use a concrete execution trace or counterexample.',answer_guidance=answer),estimated_hours=5,status='authored',prerequisite_lesson_ids=course_lessons[-2:-1],pitfalls=[answer],acceptance_focus=evidence,study_steps=[focus,'Predict the result and failure boundary before running this experiment: '+lab,'Compare observed behavior with your prediction; keep the raw trace or measurement.',challenge]))
   for aid,kind,instructions,h in [(aids[0],'guided',lab,1.5),(aids[1],'independent',challenge,2)]:
    A.append(dict(id=aid,lesson_id=lid,course_id=cid,kind=kind,title=('Laboratory: ' if kind=='guided' else 'Independent: ')+title,instructions=instructions,hours=h,acceptance_criteria=[evidence if kind=='guided' else CHALLENGE_CRITERIA[title],'Submit the implementation and a reproducible command or fixture showing the requested behavior, including one failing case.'],assessment_type='experiment' if kind=='guided' else 'independent_transfer',self_review_question=q))
  lid=cid+'-project';aid=lid+'-build';course_lessons.append(lid)
  used.add(c['lessons'][-1][2]);r=c['lessons'][-1][2]
  LS.append(dict(id=lid,course_id=cid,ordinal=len(course_lessons),type='checkpoint',title=c['title']+' — integration project',explanation=c['project'],concepts_in_context='Integrate the course concepts into an independently designed artifact. '+c['project']+' Start by writing the invariants and failure assumptions. Keep a short decision record for rejected alternatives, then demonstrate recovery as well as the happy path.',outcomes=c['projectcriteria'],reading=[dict(resource_id=f'{track}-res-{r}',priority='optional',reading_focus='Use the previously assigned reference to verify the guarantees your project claims; do not copy its example architecture without checking the failure assumptions.',estimated_minutes=30)],assignments=[aid],knowledge_check=dict(question='Which claimed guarantee has the weakest evidence in this project, and what experiment would challenge it?',self_review='Review your acceptance evidence before marking complete.',answer_guidance='Name a specific invariant, its enforcement boundary and a reproducible failure test. State remaining limitations and what would invalidate the design.'),prerequisite_lesson_ids=course_lessons[:-1],estimated_hours=20 if c['hours']>=40 else 8,status='authored',acceptance_focus=c['projectcriteria'][0]))
  A.append(dict(id=aid,lesson_id=lid,course_id=cid,kind='course_project',title=c['title']+' project',instructions=c['project'],hours=20 if c['hours']>=40 else 8,acceptance_criteria=c['projectcriteria'],assessment_type='integration_project',self_review_question='Which failure can you reproduce and recover from without manual database edits?'))
  C.append(dict(id=cid,track_id=track,title=c['title'],phase='Optional specializations' if c['optional'] else f'{1+(n-1)//4:02} · '+('Systems foundations' if track=='backend' and n<=4 else 'Engineering practice' if track=='backend' else 'Agent foundations' if n<=4 else 'Reliable agent systems'),position=n,lane='optional' if c['optional'] else 'core',recommended=False,summary=c['summary'],focus='; '.join(x[0] for x in c['lessons']),prerequisite_course_ids=[dep] if dep else [],lesson_ids=course_lessons,checkpoint_id=lid,estimated_hours=sum(x['estimated_hours'] for x in LS if x['course_id']==cid),content_status='authored'))
  if not c['optional']:previous=cid
 for r in sorted(used):
  title,url,section=RES[r];R.append(dict(id=f'{track}-res-{r}',title=title,url=url,recommended_section=section,source_type='official or primary educational source',reviewed_on='2026-10-29'))
 if track=='ai':
  # Additional normative readings belong specifically to MCP lessons.
  for r in ['mcptools','mcpauth','mcp']:
   title,url,section=RES[r];R.append(dict(id=f'ai-res-{r}',title=title,url=url,recommended_section=section,reviewed_on='2026-10-29'))
   LS[next(i for i,l in enumerate(LS) if l['id']=='ai-mcp-l02')]['reading'].append(dict(resource_id=f'ai-res-{r}',priority='required',reading_focus='Read '+section+'. Write a revision-labeled transcript and identify which requirements are transport-specific before implementing the adapter.',estimated_minutes=35))
 choices=[dict(id=track+'-branches',title='Choose depth after the core, or branch when useful',after_course_id=ids[core[-1]['slug']],option_course_ids=[ids[x['slug']] for x in optional],recommended_course_id=None,guidance='These independent specializations are optional. Check the course prerequisites and select a focus without blocking the core path. Return to another branch whenever a project creates a concrete need.')]
 M=dict(schema_version='1.0.0',id='northstar-'+track,title='Backend Engineering' if track=='backend' else 'AI & Agent Engineering',subtitle='Systems reasoning and reliable production engineering' if track=='backend' else 'Build and understand an independent agent harness',audience='Experienced Python backend engineer; no introductory syntax or CRUD prerequisites',core_course_ids=[ids[x['slug']] for x in core],optional_course_ids=[ids[x['slug']] for x in optional],first_assignment_id=A[0]['id'],first_lesson_id=LS[0]['id'],weekly_target_hours=5,learning_policy='Read, predict, experiment, implement independently and review evidence. Optional branches never gate core completion.',notes_on_time='Estimates are study budgets, not deadlines. Capstones and unfamiliar infrastructure can take substantially longer.',course_count=len(C))
 for name,value in dict(manifest=M,courses=C,lessons=LS,assignments=A,resources=R,choices=choices).items():(ROOT/'data'/track/(name+'.json')).write_text(json.dumps(value,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
 print(track,len(C),'courses',len(LS),'lessons/projects',len(A),'assignments',len(R),'resources')
if __name__=='__main__':
 compile_track('backend',B);compile_track('ai',I)
